WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.412
A ledger consumer reads payments from one partition of a Kafka topic.

2
00:00:07.412 --> 00:00:11.372
Kafka keeps the consumer's bookmark, the committed offset.

3
00:00:11.372 --> 00:00:13.557
It's 0: the next record to read.

4
00:00:13.733 --> 00:00:18.087
The consumer polls, and Kafka hands it a batch, offsets 0 to 4.

5
00:00:18.087 --> 00:00:24.307
Its position, kept in memory, jumps to 5 at once, before it has looked at a single record.

6
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:26.953
It applies each payment to the ledger.

7
00:00:26.953 --> 00:00:31.020
Then it commits 5, the next offset to read, and Kafka saves it.

8
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:32.776
It polls again: 5 to 9.

9
00:00:32.776 --> 00:00:36.546
Now it holds records that Kafka still counts as unread.

10
00:00:36.546 --> 00:00:42.783
If it crashes here, what happens depends on one choice: commit before processing, or after.

11
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.307
First, commit before processing.

12
00:00:45.307 --> 00:00:48.624
It commits 10, processes 5 and 6, and crashes.

13
00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:52.228
A new consumer starts from the committed offset, 10.

14
00:00:52.228 --> 00:00:54.866
Payments 7, 8 and 9 are never processed.

15
00:00:54.866 --> 00:00:58.228
That's at most once: nothing twice, and some never.

16
00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:01.118
Now rewind to 5 and swap the order.

17
00:01:01.118 --> 00:01:06.243
It polls 5 to 9, processes 5 and 6, and crashes before it commits.

18
00:01:06.467 --> 00:01:10.379
The new consumer starts at 5 and gets the whole batch again.

19
00:01:10.379 --> 00:01:12.987
Payments 5 and 6 reach the ledger twice.

20
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:16.680
That's at least once: nothing lost, and some records twice.

21
00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:20.808
For payments it's the right choice, because a duplicate can be caught.

22
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.404
Give each payment an idempotency key, its payment id.

23
00:01:24.404 --> 00:01:30.571
The ledger stores the key with the payment, in the same transaction, and the key must be unique.

24
00:01:30.767 --> 00:01:32.190
Replay the same crash.

25
00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:37.820
Payments 5 and 6 arrive again, their keys are already there, and the ledger skips them.

26
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:44.513
Payments 7, 8 and 9 go in, it commits 10, and every payment has landed exactly once.

27
00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:48.607
Commit after processing, so a crash replays instead of losing.

28
00:01:48.607 --> 00:01:52.009
Make the write idempotent, so the replay does no harm.
